explanation:
A class selector has higher specificity than an element selector.
```css
.label { color: teal; }
p { color: purple; }
```
A paragraph with class="label" is teal even though the p rule comes later.

When matching rules have equal specificity, the later declaration wins.
```css
.label { color: teal; }
.label { color: brown; }
```
The second rule wins because these selectors have equal specificity.

An inherited value comes from a parent. A child’s own declared color takes precedence over an inherited color.
```css
body { color: navy; }
p { color: purple; }
```
The paragraph is purple; content without its own color can inherit navy.

