explanation:
A child with z-index: 999 cannot escape a parent context below a sibling context. Raise the appropriate parent when the whole group should be above.

For this independent worked page, the teal child stays behind the silver sibling even with its large local number.

```html
<div class="stack-example"><div class="low"><span class="tag">Teal label</span></div><div class="high">Silver cover</div></div>
```

```css
.stack-example { position: relative; width: 180px; height: 80px; }
.low, .high { position: absolute; inset: 0; }
.low { z-index: 0; }
.tag { position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 500; background: teal; }
.high { z-index: 1; background: silver; }
```

Here inset: 0 sets all four offsets to zero, filling the reference box. The positioned .low and .high elements each create a context. Changing .low to z-index: 2 raises its whole group, including the child; changing only .tag cannot raise that group.

